“Claudine, men cannot be trusted. And unlike a marriage, an engagement is not a binding agreement.”
“We’re not talking about just any man, Mother. We’re talking about Matthias von Herhardt.” Claudine was calmly smiling now. “He will never love anyone other than himself.”
Claudine might have been feeling just as uneasy as her mother was if she hadn’t heard the news that Layla and the doctor’s son were going to be married. With Layla Llewellyn out of the picture, she saw no need for the excessive concern her mother was displaying.
“For him, this engagement is also a matter of family pride. There’s nothing that can get in the way of that,” she said decisively as she threaded the needle through her embroidery frame.
Kind yet cold-hearted, this was how Matthias had behaved ever since he was a child. While Claudine’s other cousins had played pranks on her and called her names, Matthias had always been polite and kind to her. This had been nice, but it had also made him seem more distant and difficult to relate to.
Sometimes she wondered if he even had emotions at all. She found it hard to picture him angry, sad, happy, or worried. It seemed more likely that he would look upon the world with the same graceful yet arrogant smile till the day he died. And this was exactly what she wanted out of him.

“I should visit Arvis next week,” she said, standing up from the sofa feeling refreshed. “I can see Duke Herhardt, and I can congratulate my friend on her upcoming marriage. Two birds with one stone.”
“A friend with an upcoming wedding? Oh no, you mean that orphan girl the gardener is taking care of? Are you really calling her your friend?” Countess Brandt asked, looking aghast.
Claudine flashed a carefree smile. “Layla is my friend. My old friend. I should get a wedding gift for her.”







After lengthy deliberation, Layla put on the white dress she had purchased for her trip to take the exam in Ratse. She neatly braided her hair, then tied it with her favorite ribbon.
She had been to the Ettmans’ home before, but she felt even more nervous than she had felt the first time, both because she and Kyle were now engaged, and because his mother had extended the invitation to her this time.
