Kyle stumbled a bit from the unexpected blow, then looked back at Bill with a frown. “But Mr. Remmer, some jerk has been talking to Layl-”
“I’m that jerk, you dummy!” “So I just think… wait, what?”
“You ill-mannered loafer, I’m going to have to seriously consider whether it’s a good idea to let you have Layla after all.” Despite his threateningly blunt words, Bill was patting Kyle’s shoulder very gently and warmly.
Layla watched from a few steps back as the two men squabbled.
Those two men were the two people she loved most in the whole world. The two most precious people to her. The two people she never wanted to

lose. A smile slowly formed on her face and joy suffused her heart. Uncle Bill and Kyle always had that effect on her. She couldn’t imagine life without them. This was why she had finally decided to marry Kyle, after hesitating for so long. With each day that he had proposed to her and she had rejected him, things had grown more awkward between them. If she couldn’t accept his love, then it was only right for a distance to form between them. He had tried as hard as he could to close that gap, but since he had already proposed to her, there were only two options remaining to them; get married or stop seeing
each other entirely.
Her wish for them to remain good friends, while becoming no more than that, had become impossible the moment he had first proposed to her. Deep down she had known this, and it had pained her throughout the previous autumn, winter, and spring.
Marriage, and all the rest of the future life that Kyle had talked to her about, had seemed like a vague, far-off notion to her. She didn’t like the idea of excessive desire either. But if marriage was the only way she could avoid losing Kyle, she thought she could find the courage for it.
She had made this resolution in the late spring, the same day that Duke Herhardt had insulted her and made her cry. Not wanting to hold onto anything he had given her, she had thrown all his gold coins into the fountain. The moment she had looked into Kyle’s eyes after he came looking for her, she had made up her mind.
The look in his eyes had made her forget all her pain and sorrow. It reminded her of the cozy light of the cottage. So many times, walking down the dark forest path at night, she had seen the warm light of the cottage and had felt deep reassurance knowing that Bill was there waiting for her, ready to comfort her from whatever pain or sadness the world had inflicted on her.

That evening by the fountain, Kyle’s eyes had given her the same warm, cozy feeling.
As always, Kyle and Bill’s meaningless squabble ended with both of them laughing heartily. Still chuckling, Kyle turned and extended his hand towards her. “Let’s go, Layla.”
Hesitating slightly, she lightly grasped the tip of his fingers. He chuckled and regripped her hand fully. Her tiny hand was practically swallowed up by his.
While she still wasn’t used to these new displays of affection that had started after she had accepted his proposal, she didn’t shake off his hand. She didn’t want to lose him.




32.

Happy Days.






Mrs. Ettman welcomed her guest with a table packed with delicious- looking dishes and beautiful decorations. Not only Layla, but even Dr.
Ettman and Kyle were amazed by the warm reception.
“Thank you for inviting me, Mrs. Ettman,” Layla said. As she recited this greeting, which she had practiced dozens of times throughout the day, she offered Mrs. Ettman the gifts she had brought; a bouquet of fresh roses, and an ornate jar filled with pickled peaches.
“Why thank you,” Mrs. Ettman said, accepting the gifts.
Layla smiled, feeling relieved that this first step had gone well. Seeing the smile on her face, Kyle allowed a smile to break through his serious demeanor. When Mrs. Ettman noticed that her son was smiling, her face went cold momentarily, before returning to an expression of warm hospitality.

