Rather than telling her how silly she sounded, he decided to stop talking about the bag, and instead held out the acceptance letter in front of her face.
“What’s this, Uncle Bill?” “Read it and find out.”
She gave him a surprised glance, then took the letter. Rather than bursting into ecstatic celebration as he had expected her to, as she read the letter she seemed to grow calmer and more relaxed.
Eventually, he grew concerned enough by her unusually calm reaction that he called to her. “Layla…?”
Only then did she lift her head and look at him. She had a reserved smile on her face.
It would be nice if you acted a bit more like a young lady at times like this, Bill thought, scratching the back of his neck in embarrassment over the fact that she had reacted to the news in a more adult-like manner than he had.
Watching his reaction, she started smiling even more brightly. Before he even had time to acknowledge her smile, she had wrapped her arms around him joyfully.
“No need to overreact,” he said. Despite his indifferent words, however, he lovingly patted her on the back.
After a while, she looked up at him and whispered, “Thank you.” Her eyes were welling up with tears. “Thank you so much, Uncle Bill.” She smiled brightly at him. “It’s all thanks to you, Uncle Bill.”
“That’s ridiculous,” he replied. Taking a deep breath, he tried to distract himself by mentally counting up all the tasks he had to finish before sundown. Even so, his eyes felt like they were ready to burst into tears again. Why is she so good at making me cry? “You’re the one who studied, you’re the one who did so well on the exam. What did I do?”

“No. No, Uncle Bill.” Shaking her head vigorously, she grabbed his hand, barely able to fit both her hands around one of his. “I…” Her hands felt soft and warm as they gripped his. “If it weren’t for you, Uncle Bill, I would have…” On this wonderful day, she looked like she was about to start sobbing. This frightened Bill more than anything. He didn’t like seeing her shedding tears,
even if they were tears of joy.
He only ever wanted to see a cheerful smile on her face. All the love and care he had put into all the flowers and trees he had cultivated throughout his whole life, didn’t equal the love he felt for this girl. There was no flower or tree that he cherished more than her.
He wasn’t sure when it had gotten that way. One day he had simply realized that it was true.
Clearing his throat to calm his emotions, he said, “Let’s go to the capital next weekend, what do you say?”
“You and me, to Ratse?” she asked, looking surprised.
“Now that you’ve been admitted to the university, you’ve got to pay the tuition and enroll in classes, right? While we’re there, we can do some sightseeing. It’ll be nice. After all, I’ve never taken you anywhere before.”
