“It won’t be a fancy trip or anything. Mainly we’re just going to pay the tuition.” “Even so.”
As he saw how excited she was about the trip, he felt regret. It was then that Chef Mona’s words about him not knowing how to take care of a daughter hit home. He wondered why he had never taken her on any sort of trip, even a short trip somewhere close where she could see some sights and he

could buy her some good food to eat. He wondered why this thought was only now occurring to him, when she would be moving away so soon.
“The tuition is a lot of money, and anyway I’m not comfortable sending you and Kyle off together without a chaperone again, even if you’re going to get married. So I have no choice but to bring you myself…” He continued to mutter excuses until he felt embarrassed and laughed at himself.
Layla hugged him again.
“Hey, Layla, I was right, wasn’t I?” he said, softly patting her head with a delighted smile on his face. “Didn’t I say you would become an outstanding adult?” Although this comment failed to fully convey his emotions, he gave up on trying to find more words and quietly continued patting her head. To prevent himself from being overcome by sobs, he started mentally counting up his tasks once again.




33.

Arvis’s Heaven.






Word spread quickly through Arvis that Layla, the girl that Bill Remmer was raising, had gotten into Ratse University.
Simultaneously, the word was spreading that Dr. Ettman’s only son had been admitted into the same university’s medical school after passing the entrance exam with flying colors, but this news didn’t generate as much buzz, since everyone had already expected it to happen.
Layla’s news was a different story, however. For several days, whenever two or more people met, Layla Llewellyn would inevitably become a topic of their conversation. This was even true of conversations that went on in the duke’s greenhouse.
“To think, a common orphan, and a female one at that, going to college. Bill Remmer must have done an amazing job raising her,” said Dowager

Herhardt. Normally she wouldn’t participate in gossiping about a lowly child, but today she was displaying just as much interest in Layla as anyone.
“She must have been born lucky. First, she met her savior, Bill, and then she managed to get engaged to Dr. Ettman’s son,” Elise von Herhardt added.
Claudine, who had been quietly sipping her tea, politely smiled and nodded. “I’m so happy that poor girl has had such good luck.” She felt more capable than ever of sincerely praising Layla.
