He glanced at Layla for a moment, then lightly nodded his head.
After exchanging a few more pleasantries and salutations, Layla was finally free to leave this uncomfortable tea table. As she was walking away from the Herhardts, she was able to take in the interior of the greenhouse.
Everyone who visited it praised it enthusiastically, calling it Arvis’s heaven. Now that Layla was able to look around the lavish greenhouse, she felt uneasy. Seeing all the beautiful birds with clipped wings, and all the excessively colorful and fragrant flowers, she started to feel overwhelmed. This feeling was heightened by the sound of the water flowing in the marble fountain, and
by the sunlight that was intensified as it shone through the glass walls.
She left the greenhouse immediately, not looking back once. Only once she was outside, in the gentle embrace of the breeze and the natural sunlight, was

she able to breathe a sigh of relief.
Her long evening shadow followed her as she walked back towards the woods.







“Linda, do you know what you’re saying right now?” Daniel Raynor asked in disbelief. The rays of the setting sun flowing in through the uncurtained window made him squint as he sat across from his cousin, Linda Ettman. “Linda!”
