The leaves rustled under his shoes as he walked.

When he heard the sound of a bicycle in the distance, he slowed his pace even more.
Eventually, he could hear what he was sure was Layla coming to a stop a moderate distance behind him. He then turned around, and just as he expected, he saw Layla standing next to her bicycle.
She glanced around for a moment, then curtsied and awkwardly said, “Hello, Your Grace.”
The braided bun in her hair had started to come undone, and the loose strands were swaying in the breeze. He had noticed that she didn’t seem to have a talent for doing her hair—it was always either tied up too tightly or, like today, too loosely. He didn’t mind her sloppy appearance, however.
He bowed his head at her, putting one hand behind his straightened back. She blinked her widened eyes, gripping her bicycle’s handlebars tightly. He hadn’t done anything, and yet she had a noticeably uneasy look on her face.
“Well… please excuse me, then,” she said. Curtsying to him once more, she walked her bicycle past him.
Just as she had gotten back onto the bicycle and was about to start pedaling, he said, “You should act like a lady, Layla.”
She stopped.
“You know. If you want me to be a gentleman, then you have to act like a lady.”
She remained in place, not getting down from the bicycle but not pedaling either.
He slowly walked towards her. “And your pigeon will only be safe if I’m a gentleman.”
She turned to face him after he made this comment. After glaring at him for a moment, she got off the bicycle.

He cocked his head slightly as he looked at her. Her moist eyes looked frightened, but the twisted expression on her lips was full of defiant annoyance at this situation. What an attitude she has, he thought.
After a moment of staring at her as she trembled over his joking remark about her pigeon, he casually walked past her in the direction of the mansion. She followed behind him, looking at her feet and walking her bicycle with her.
“I didn’t tell you to act like you’re my maid,” he muttered, glancing over his shoulder.
She looked up with surprise. “Pardon? Ah…” Despite her stubbornness, she grasped what he was saying. With a reluctant look on her face, she took a few steps closer to him, but she remained one step behind.
With a laugh, he took one step back, thus getting what he wanted. He started to walk again, and she carefully matched his pace. With a bicycle between them, the two walked down Platanus Road, side by side.
They didn’t exchange any words as they walked. The only sounds were her bicycle’s wheels turning, the leaves crunching under their feet, and the cool breeze blowing through the trees.
He walked quite slowly for how long his legs were. She glanced at him after noticing that there was still a long way to go despite how long they had been walking. He had always been the unhurried type, but today he seemed to be walking particularly slowly. Of course, she realized that she might be feeling this way because of how awkward and uncomfortable the situation was.
He was about the same height as Kyle but was a much more imposing figure than him. Perhaps this was because of his straight, elegant posture, as well as his piercing eyes. As she continued to look at him, she noticed that his

frame was bigger and more muscular than Kyle’s. Perhaps that was why he seemed more domineering.
As she considered these possible explanations, her gaze gradually moved upward. She observed his pale, smooth hands, his dark-dray coat, his exquisitely- knotted tie, his expressionless lips, and… his eyes.
As she looked at those blue eyes staring back at her, she was hit with an instinctual feeling of fear. She could have rid herself of the fear by simply lowering her head, but for some reason, she found it hard to do so.
As she stood, transfixed by his eyes, he slowly started to speak.




49.

Like a Bird’s Wings.






“That hairstyle doesn’t suit you,” the duke said.
Layla was taken aback by his comment. It took her a moment to process what he had said. Once she did, she felt even more confused. Why was he making such a remark out of the blue? “Yes,” she replied in a cold tone, “I know my hair is a little… unkempt right now.”
“A little?” he scoffed, looking her up and down.
She squeezed her bicycle’s handlebars tighter, fighting the urge to flee. But she couldn’t stop the anger that was welling up inside her. She was embarrassed to feel her cheeks starting to turn red.
“You might as well just wear your hair down,” he said.
“I’d like to, but I was told that wouldn’t be appropriate for a teacher.” “Who told you that?”

“The principal.” She felt her face growing increasingly warmer, so finally, she broke eye contact with the duke. The silence that ensued made her feel even more awkward, so she continued. “Apparently I look too young, so in order to maintain my authority as a teacher, I’m supposed to avoid hairstyles that make me look like a student.”
She heard the sound of the duke chuckling amid the sound of the breeze, but she didn’t turn back to look at him. Now she felt her ears heating up as well. She was sure she looked ridiculous.
“You’re saying that disheveled bun is supposed to bring you authority as a teacher…?” he asked sarcastically. Looking down at her, he was pleased to see a prim expression that suggested her pride had been hurt.
“I’m getting a lot of practice putting up my hair every day, so I’m sure I’ll get better at it soon.
Wow, she never gives in, does she? He snickered. “Hmm, you think so?” “Yes, I do. If I find that I’m unable to improve, I’ll cut my hair short.” “Cut it short?” he asked. The smile left his face and his eyes narrowed.
She nodded solemnly at his brash question. “Yes. That would make me look much more mature, and…”
“Don’t cut it,” he calmly said.
Puzzled by what she had just heard, she looked at him and angrily asked, “Do you think I need your permission to cut my hair or let it grow?”
“It’s pretty,” he gently replied.
She could hardly believe her ears. She stared at him, and he stared right back, looking utterly calm.
“It’s like a bird’s wings.”
His voice sounded exactly the same as it did when he was insulting her.

Reflecting on this, she realized that he had always spoken in a soft, gentle tone, even when saying hurtful things to her. This made her think back to the day they had first met. Even on that summer day all those years ago, when she had been terrified after almost being shot by him, his voice had been quiet and gentle, just like now.
She vividly recalled frantically climbing down the tree, running back to the cottage, and shouting to Bill in a panicked voice, “There’s someone in the woods! A tall man! He had black hair and blue eyes, his voice was as smooth as a duck’s feathers.”
In that moment of panic, as she had hurried to find words to describe the duke’s voice, the first thing that had come to mind was duck feathers—those soft, iridescent feathers that she had collected as if they were precious gems scattered along the side of the Schulter River.
She quickly averted her eyes again. She would have preferred it if he had simply said something cruel to her. That was the Duke Herhardt she knew—a frightening, imposing, despicable man.
She kept waiting, but he said nothing more. Instead, he simply walked on, looking at the leaf-strewn road. She started to wonder if the strange compliment she thought she had heard had been her imagination.
She felt at her hair, which had continued to come undone, then shook her head, frowning. Since she had taken her hands off her bicycle’s handlebars, it started to sway. She grabbed at the handlebars again, but it was too late; the bicycle fell to the ground, pulling her with it.
The sounds of the bicycle crashing and her crying out echoed through the quiet road.
Matthias looked back at the ridiculous scene, furrowing his brow slightly. Seeing Layla pinned under her bicycle, he couldn’t help but chuckle. “You seem

to fall a lot,” he said.
She bit her lip. She felt humiliated, and yet, at the same time, she felt relieved. She silently waited for him to ridicule her and leave.
But instead, he just quietly lifted her bicycle up for her. Then he walked over to where her bag had fallen, picked it up, then walked back and crouched down in front of her.
Before he could pick up any more of her scattered belongings, she snatched the bag from him and said, “Don’t. I’ll get those.”
He raised his eyebrows at her open hostility.
“I… I’ll pick up the rest of the things,” she muttered. Looking down, she began to retrieve the things she had dropped.
He noticed that her hands were trembling, even though he hadn’t said anything to frighten her.
He felt irritated, but he decided to keep watching her. When he noticed her flushed cheeks and neck, it occurred to him that her hands might just be trembling from embarrassment rather than fear. That wouldn’t be so bad.
He stood up again. Under his long shadow, she continued to scramble to pick up everything she had dropped. He realized how distraught she was when he saw that she had also distractedly picked up some stray pebbles and leaves. This cleared away all the irritation he had been feeling.
After picking everything up and putting it all back in her bag, she stood up and faced him. She seemed to have forgotten about the dirt stains on her clothes and hands.
“I know it’s rude, Your Grace, but…” Her eyes darted back and forth between him and the mansion’s gate down the road, “…but if it’s rude for me to walk ahead of you, I’ll wait for you to go ahead first.”

She didn’t want to walk with him anymore. Even as she said these bold words, an anxious, uneasy look remained in her eyes.
Despite her irritating attitude, he readily assented to her request. He knew that they couldn’t walk together past the gate, anyway.
“Go ahead,” he said.
“Me? I can go first?” she asked, looking surprised. He nodded.
Looking relieved, she bowed her head. “Thank you, Your Grace.”
He felt almost insulted by the polite attitude she had suddenly adopted.
She started walking ahead. Before getting back on her bicycle, she glanced back at him. Despite the kindness he had shown her, she still felt suspicious. She tilted her head a bit, narrowed her eyes, tilted her head some more, then finally climbed onto her bicycle and rode off.
Maybe I should’ve just made her cry he thought, feeling some slight regret.
Soon, with a relatively content expression on his face, he started walking.
Just then, he noticed a shiny pen on the road. In a graceful motion, he stooped over and picked up the pen that she had dropped as she fell. She was still close enough to hear him if he had chosen to call out to her, but he didn’t feel like doing that.
Twirling the pen between his long fingers, he sauntered down the road.
Swiftly pedaling, she soon passed through Arvis’s gates and out of his sight.







“The bird’s here again,” Mark Evers said, smiling. Matthias didn’t have to turn to look through the window to know which bird he was talking about— Phoebe. It was the usual visiting time of that bird that was so much smarter and more obedient than its owner.
Sitting on a chair by the fireplace, Matthias signed the last document, bringing the day’s work to a close. His assistant took the documents and left, leaving him alone in the drawing room.
He placed the cap back on the fountain pen and looked out the window. As usual, Phoebe was perched on the railing, busily pecking at her feed.
Matthias shifted his gaze from the bird to the slender pen in his hand. The name of the pen’s owner was inscribed on the cap in gold lettering: Layla Llewellyn.
It looked new, and it didn’t seem like something she would have bought herself. Perhaps it was a gift from the gardener. He hoped it was. If it was a gift from Bill Remmer, she would do anything she could to get it back.
She was probably realizing it was gone right about now.
Gazing contemplatively at the pen, he removed its cap again. Perhaps it would be nice to do a gentlemanly act for the woman who had praised him as Karlsbar’s greatest gentleman. Besides, the time had come for the pigeon to pay for all the food it had eaten.
He wrote a short message on a small strip of paper. Folding the paper, he walked out onto the balcony. The pigeon remained still as he tied the note to

its leg. Matthias chuckled as he thought of the contrast between this calm bird and the frantic way its owner had ridden away on her bicycle.
Once the note was securely tied, he tossed the bird up in the air, and it immediately started flying straight towards the cottage.







Phoebe arrived just as night was falling. Layla was sitting absentmindedly at her empty desk when she heard a pecking sound at her window. “Phoebe!”
She quickly wrapped a shawl over her shoulders and stood up. When she opened the window, a frigid breeze blew in. “You must be hungry. I’ll go get you some delicious… huh?”
Her eyes widened at the sight of the note tied to Phoebe’s leg. Thinking she must be seeing things, she rubbed her eyes, but there was definitely a note. It was something that hadn’t happened, that couldn’t have happened, since Kyle had left.
“Kyle…?” she whispered, even though she knew it was impossible that Kyle had sent the note.
After staring blankly at Phoebe’s leg for a moment, she untied the string with trembling hands and unfolded the note. As she read the single sentence, the look on her face instantly shifted from curiosity to dismay.
She gasped and took a step backward. The note slipped from her fingers, fluttering to the floor in front of the window.
Staring down at the note, she took another step back, and another, until she bumped into the dresser behind her.

“It can’t be,” she muttered in a shaky voice.
She hurriedly started searching through her bag, but she couldn’t find the pen. She was flustered even more when she found several pebbles and leaves that had somehow gotten in there.
No longer able to deny what had happened, she staggered back to the window. She picked up the note with a hand as pale as the moonlight. She read it once more.
Where s your pen, Layla ?
She moaned and clutched her hair. “It can’t be. It can’t be,” she muttered, but she knew it was no use denying it.
Meanwhile, Phoebe, well-fattened by the duke, cooed calmly in the pale autumn moonlight.




50.

For My Precious Cousin.


